        "width": 736,
        "height": 1360,
        "content": "## Two different people must approve a change request\n\nChange requests land in an Airtable table. This workflow asks two different approvers by email, one\nafter the other, and marks the request approved only when both say yes. The person who raised the\nrequest is never asked to approve it.\n\n### How it works\nA new row starts a run. Each row is handled once, so a row polled twice never emails anyone twice,\nand requests a human has already decided are skipped.\n\nThe approver pool is read from a second Airtable table. The requester's own address is removed from\nit, duplicate addresses collapse to one person, and the first two survivors are chosen. If fewer\nthan two are left the request is refused, never approved by default.\n\nEach approver gets a Gmail message carrying Approve and Disapprove buttons. Three answers are\nhandled rather than two: approved, refused, and no answer inside the reply window. The second\napprover is asked only after the first one approves.\n\nThe decision, both approver addresses and the reason for any refusal are written back to the row,\nand the requester is emailed the outcome.\n\n### Setup\n1. In Airtable, create a **Change Requests** table with Request ID, Title, Detail, Requester Email,\n   Status (Pending / Approved / Refused), First Approver, Second Approver, Refusal Reason, Decided\n   At, and a **Created** field of type *Created time*. The trigger polls on Created, so a row with\n   that cell empty is never picked up. Create an **Approvers** table with Email and Active.\n2. Connect an Airtable personal access token and a Gmail account.\n3. Pick your base and table on each of the four Airtable nodes.\n4. Open **Approval Rules** to set the reply window, the status labels and whether a requester may\n   approve their own request."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
